Z||000007||Philphine||08-31-2001||02:51 PM||||for a while jc whitney had some that had a momentary switch for a horn and an on/off switch for an asessory, but they stopped selling them. anyone know where to get something like that?||||reg||1
Z||000008||DKOV||08-31-2001||03:46 PM||||Your Local auto parts store will have that sort of thing hanging on the shelf.  Of course, Radio Shack carries thoes too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||7
Z||000009||rogergarrison||08-31-2001||03:56 PM||||I went next door to a machine shop. He had a catalog with all sorts of parts for machinery. They have all sorts of handles on their machines like milling machines etc. Listed are all sizes of round knobs to screw onto them, I used one till I got my Ferrari style conversion kit. all you need is the shaft size, thread pitch and the size ball you want. It was only like $4 for a 2"||||reg||1
Z||000010||StuGood||08-31-2001||04:14 PM||||This thread reminds me of when the shift knob came off my Chevy Monza.  It was a plastic threaded shift knob, that held the reverse lockout spring under it.  It was pretty old  cracked, and starting to get loose, but of course I just kept using it.   <P>So one day I was driving along, and the knob split and came off the shifter... the reverse lockout spring squirted out and landed in the back of the car somewhere.  Was near an auto parts store, so pulled in and looked for one of those "universal" replacement shift knobs - the kind with a set screw.  The shifter on that Monza was pretty thick (7/8" diameter or something), and none of those replacement knobs was going to work.<P>Back home, I found the reverse lockout spring, but what to do for a knob?  Thought about making a knob and threading it, but the cost of a 7/8" x (whatever thread) tap would have been way too much, especially since I'd likely never use it again for anything else.<P>I finally used the fancy wooden cap from a cologne bottle (English Leather, I think?).    Epoxied a short length of steel pipe into the cap, leaving about 1/4" of pipe sticking out the bottom of the cap.  Tapped 3 little set screw holes around the periphery of the pipe, and used headless set screws to hold the knob on.  Worked great   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 08-31-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000011||Mickey_Moose||08-31-2001||05:58 PM||||Well to ways:<P>get one with a hole that is too small - drill it out and tap it with the correct thread size.<P>cut the shaft and thread it to fit the ball - this will also give you the shorter shifter that so many people like  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||BlackDragon||08-31-2001||08:45 PM||||i could do it, i made an 8ball shifter for my old 82 rx-7 before it completely died. it took about 30 minutes. not that hard, just need the tools. tap and die, and drill bit<P>------------------<BR>im looking for a inexpensive black standard fastback. does not have to run. does not have to look good. only the mid section needs to be dent free. the fenders,bumpers, dont have to be in good cond. faded paint is fine. anyone who has info, email me. grey interior would be nice but beggars cant be choosers||||reg||1
